_Then, for \(h\leqslant h_{1}\) small enough, there exists a constant \(K^{\prime}>0,\) independent of \(h\) and \(\lambda,\) such that:_

(3.51) \[\|u_{h}(\lambda)-u(\lambda)\|_{H}\leqslant K^{\prime}\{\|(T-T_{h})G(\lambda,u( \lambda))\|_{H}+\|u_{h}(\lambda)-u(\lambda)\|_{X}^{2}\}.\]

Proof.: First, in view of (3.47) and (3.49), observe that:

\[\|D_{u}F(\lambda,u(\lambda))-D_{u}F_{h}(\lambda,u(\lambda))\|_{ \mathscr{L}(H;H)} =\|(T-T_{h})D_{u}G(\lambda,u(\lambda))\|_{\mathscr{L}(H;H)}\] \[\leqslant\|T-T_{h}\|_{\mathscr{L}(Y;H)}\|D_{u}G(\lambda,u(\lambda ))\|_{\mathscr{L}(H;Y)}.\]

Therefore, (3.48) implies that

\[\limsup_{h\to 0}\sup_{\lambda\in A}\|D_{u}F(\lambda,u(\lambda))-D_{u}F_{h}( \lambda,u(\lambda))\|_{\mathscr{L}(H;H)}=0.\]

Hence it stems from (3.50) and Lemma 3.3 that for all sufficiently small \(h\) and all \(\lambda\) in \(A,\)\(D_{u}F_{h}(\lambda,u(\lambda))\) is an isomorphism of \(H\) with:

\[\|[D_{u}F_{h}(\lambda,u(\lambda))]^{-1}\|_{\mathscr{L}(H;H)}\leqslant C_{1}.\]

Then, like in Theorem 3.1, we can write:

\[u(\lambda)-u_{h}(\lambda) =[D_{u}F_{h}(\lambda,u(\lambda))]^{-1}\big{[}T_{h}\{D_{u}G(\lambda ,u(\lambda))\cdot(u(\lambda)-u_{h}(\lambda))-(G(\lambda,u(\lambda))\] \[-G(\lambda,u_{h}(\lambda))\}+F_{h}(\lambda,u(\lambda))].\]

But

\[G(\lambda,u(\lambda))-G(\lambda,u_{h}(\lambda))-D_{u}G(\lambda,u( \lambda))\cdot(u(\lambda)-u_{h}(\lambda))\] (3.52) \[=-\bigg{]}_{0}^{1}(1-t)D_{uu}^{2}G(\lambda,(1-t)u(\lambda)+tu_{h} (\lambda))\,dt\cdot(u(\lambda)-u_{h}(\lambda))^{2}.\]

Therefore the boundedness assumption on \(D^{2}G\) together with (3.48) yield:

\[\|u(\lambda)-u_{h}(\lambda)\|_{H}\leqslant C_{1}[C_{2}\|u(\lambda)-u_{h}( \lambda)\|_{X}^{2}+\|F_{h}(\lambda,u(\lambda))-F(\lambda,u(\lambda))\|_{H}],\]

which proves (3.51). 

### Non-Differentiable Approximation of Branches of Nonsingular Solutions

So far, we have assumed that the approximate mapping \(F_{h}\) retained the smoothness properties of the mapping \(F,\) because the approximation was performed on the linear operator \(T\) alone and not on \(G.\) But it is sometimes necessary to approximate \(G\) by a mapping \(G_{h}\) which is no longer differentiable. This occurs, for example, when an upwind discretization of the convective terms is used in the Navier-Stokes equations. This situation is analyzed by the following theory, which is an easy variant of the one elaborated in Sections 3.2 and 3.3. The reader can refer to Rappaz [67] for a more general approach that encompasses all the material of this paragraph.
